text:
The Observed Statistic
Notice that the stance variable has 3 levels - Yes, No, Not sure. In order to run inference for a single proportion, we need the stance variable to have only two levels. To do this, we can group No and Not sure together and call them Not Yes. See the code below:

Codedream_filtered &lt;- dream |&gt;
  mutate(stance = if_else(stance == "Yes", "Yes", "Not Yes"))


Then, we create the summary table again using the filtered dataset:

Codedream_filtered |&gt;
  group_by(stance) |&gt;
  summarize(count = n()) |&gt;
  mutate(proportion = count / sum(count))

# A tibble: 2 × 3
  stance  count proportion
  &lt;chr&gt;   &lt;int&gt;      &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Not Yes   436      0.479
2 Yes       474      0.521


Thus, the observed statistic is 0.521 (or 52.1%). This is the proportion of Americans in the sample that supported the DREAM Act in 2012.

Running Simulations
Next, we want to run simulations to see the distribution of the observed statistic under the null hypothesis. We will use the infer package to do this. Run the code below:

Codeset.seed(1234) # Prevents simulated data from changing.
dream_sim &lt;- dream_filtered |&gt;
  specify(response = stance, success = "Yes") |&gt;
  hypothesize(null = "point", p = 0.54) |&gt;
  generate(reps = 500, type = "draw") |&gt;
  calculate(stat = "prop")


The code should run without errors. The dream_sim object (see environment) contains 500 simulated proportions under the null hypothesis.

Confidence Interval
To create a 95% confidence interval, we use our filtered data to run bootstrap simulations to create several bootstrap samples:

Codeset.seed(12) # Prevents simulated data from changing.
dream_boot &lt;- dream_filtered |&gt;
  specify(response = stance, success = "Yes") |&gt;
  generate(reps = 500, type = "bootstrap") |&gt;
  calculate(stat = "prop")


We can visualize the bootstrap distribution using a histogram:

Codedream_boot |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = stat)) +
  geom_histogram(bins=55)







Notice that the bootstrap distribution is centered around the observed statistic (approx. 0.52). Next, we can use the get_ci() function to create a 95% confidence interval as shown below:

Codedream_boot |&gt;
  get_ci(level = 0.95, type = "percentile")

# A tibble: 1 × 2
  lower_ci upper_ci
     &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;dbl&gt;
1    0.488    0.552


The 95% confidence interval is approximately (0.488 to 0.552) or 48.8% to 55.2%. This means that we are 95% confident that the true proportion of Americans who supported the DREAM Act in 2012 is between 48.8% and 55.2%. This interval further supports the conclusion reached for the hypothesis test since the value of 54% falls inside this confidence interval.

Observed Statistic
The observed statistic in this scenario would be the difference in average weight lost between the incentive and the control group. The code below drops the missing values from the WightLoss variable, then groups by Group and calculate the mean for each group:

CodeWeightLossIncentive |&gt;
  drop_na(WeightLoss) |&gt;
  group_by(Group) |&gt;
  summarize(mean(WeightLoss))

# A tibble: 2 × 2
  Group     `mean(WeightLoss)`
  &lt;fct&gt;                  &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Control                 3.92
2 Incentive              15.7 


The observed statistic is:
\[\text{Observed Statistic} = 15.68 - 3.92 = 11.76 \text{ pounds}.\]

Running Simulations
Next, we want to run simulations to generate the null distribution. We will use the infer package functions to do this. See the code below:

Codeset.seed(12) # Prevents simulated data from changing.
null_distribution &lt;- WeightLossIncentive |&gt;
  specify(WeightLoss ~ Group) |&gt;
  hypothesize(null = "independence") |&gt;
  generate(reps = 1000, type = "permute") |&gt;
  calculate(stat = "diff in means", order = c("Incentive", "Control"))


Upon running the code the null_distribution object should appear under the environment area. This contains 1000 simulated difference in means under the assumption that the null hypothesis is true(hence centered around 0).

Codenull_distribution |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = stat)) +
  geom_histogram(binwidth = 2, fill = "#4963D4", color = "white")+
  geom_vline(xintercept = 11.76, color = "red")






Code# The bars are filled using the color code "#4963D4". Google html color codes.


The vertical line to the right represents the observed statistic(11.76 pounds) calculated earlier.

Confidence Interval
Next, we want to create a confidence interval to estimate the true difference in average 4-month weight loss between the two groups. First, we run a bootstrap simulation from the sample. You may want to review the class activities for week 11(Mon and Wed) if you need to refresh your mind on the physical mechanics of this process.

Codeset.seed(123) # Prevents simulated data from changing.
bootstrap_dist &lt;- WeightLossIncentive |&gt;
  specify(WeightLoss ~ Group) |&gt;
  generate(reps = 1000, type = "bootstrap") |&gt;
  calculate(stat = "diff in means", order = c("Incentive", "Control"))


The above code runs 1000 simulations of the difference in means using bootstrap resampling. Since we are resampling from the observed data, the bootstrap distribution will be centered around the observed statistic of 11.76 pounds. We can create a histogram to verify this. Examine then run the code below:

Codebootstrap_dist |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = stat)) +
  geom_histogram(binwidth = 1.6,fill="darkgreen", color="black")






Code# Here, the bars are filled using "darkgreen".


Next, we use the get_ci() function from the infer package to find the 95% confidence interval:

Codebootstrap_dist |&gt;
  get_ci(level = 0.95, type = "percentile")

# A tibble: 1 × 2
  lower_ci upper_ci
     &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;dbl&gt;
1     5.75     17.4


The lower and upper bounds of the confidence interval are approximately 5.75 and 17.38 pounds, respectively.

Bootstrap Simulations
We want to run a simulation process known as bootsrapping to create a confidence interval for the true population parameter (the true difference in promotion rates between the sexes in the population).
Examine and run the code below:

Codeset.seed(123) #This prevents simulated data from changing.
bootstrap_diffs &lt;- sex_discrimination |&gt;
  specify(decision~sex, success = "promoted") |&gt;
  generate(reps = 1000, type = "bootstrap") |&gt;
  calculate(stat = "diff in props", order=c("male","female"))


